    window.ASSESS_NARR = {
outcomes_principles:[
'When you face an ethical decision, you start from its consequences. You judge a choice by what it will actually do to the people involved, weighing the harm it causes against the good. Your strength is that you stay flexible and humane. Your risk is that a persuasive calculation of the benefits can talk you across a line you should not cross.',
'You weigh consequences and principles against each other and let each one check the other. That makes you hard to push into blind rule-following or pure expediency. In a fast-moving decision, though, you may stall over which of the two should win.',
'When you face an ethical decision, you start from your principles. For you, some lines hold no matter how good the payoff for crossing them would be. Your strength is that you are dependable and hard to corrupt with an attractive result. Your risk is that you may keep defending a rule past the point where the rule itself is doing real harm.'],
scrutiny_trust:[
'When you face an ethical decision, you start by questioning your own motives. You assume that some of your reasoning serves your own interests until you have checked it. Your strength is that you are hard to fool, including by yourself. Your risk is paralysis: too much self-doubt can leave you distrusting judgments that were actually sound.',
'You question yourself enough to stay honest and you trust yourself enough to act. Your risk is defaulting to whichever of the two feels easier on the day.',
'When you face an ethical decision, you trust your own moral judgment. You read the situation and act on that reading without endless second-guessing. Your strength is clarity and conviction. Your risk is that the one motive you never examined turns out to be the blind spot.'],
decisive_deliberative:[
'You make ethical decisions quickly. Once you see the situation, you make the call and move, because sitting in ambiguity feels like avoidance. Your strength is that you do not leave hard calls hanging. Your risk is that you settle the question before you have seen the whole situation.',
'You give an ethical decision the time it needs, but not more. You can hold a question open without rushing to settle it, and you do not let it drift unresolved. Your risk is misjudging the moments when speed genuinely matters.',
'You take your time over ethical decisions. You stay with the complexity and resist settling the question too early. Your strength is that you are thorough and rarely blindsided. Your risk is that while you deliberate, the delay becomes a decision of its own, made without you ever choosing it.'],
broad_defined:[
'You take a wide view of what you are responsible for. If you can see a problem, you treat it as partly yours to deal with, whether or not it sits inside your role. Your strength is that very little slips past you unaddressed. Your risk is overreach: stepping in where the decision rightly belongs to someone else.',
'You take responsibility for what is yours and flag what is not, without either hiding from problems or taking them over. Cases that sit in the gray zone between the two can still catch you flat-footed.',
'You keep to the responsibilities of your own role. You respect the limits of your standing and other people\u2019s right to make their own calls. Your strength is focus, and you do not trespass on other people\u2019s ground. Your risk is that \u201Cnot my call\u201D becomes a place to hide from problems you could help with.'],
candor_diplomatic:[
'When an ethical situation calls for a hard truth, you say what needs to be said plainly and directly. Your strength is that people always know where they stand with you. Your risk is that bluntness lands as a blow, and the person stops listening before the message arrives.',
'You tell the truth, but you choose the moment and the words. Your risk is that the right moment keeps receding until it never arrives.',
'When an ethical situation calls for a hard truth, you deliver it with tact and careful timing. Your strength is that you keep the relationship, and the door, open. Your risk is that you soften the message until the point of it is lost.']
};
